<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/expert/global-governance">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Global governance is <strong>the management of shared problems without a world
    government</strong> — institutions, treaties and norms coordinating sovereign states that
    answer to no higher power. Its central tension: effectiveness requires binding commitments;
    sovereignty forbids them. Everything in the field is a workaround for that tension.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the architecture, the assessment, the climate test, the current stress" widget="equationStep" config={globgovCfg} />

  <h2>The design patterns that work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Clubs and conditional cooperation:</strong> the EU (deep integration among willing members), trade agreements with enforcement — smaller circles, deeper rules, exit allowed.</li>
    <li><strong>Monitoring and reputation:</strong> treaties work better with transparency (nuclear inspections, IMF surveillance) — information substitutes weakly for enforcement (the international-law lesson's compliance logic).</li>
    <li><strong>Standards bodies are the quiet giants:</strong> ICAO (aviation), ITU (telecom), WIPO, ISO — technical coordination where politics stays out and planes land on time everywhere. Global governance\'s greatest hits rarely make headlines.</li>
    <li><strong>NGOs and multi-stakeholder models:</strong> NGOs, corporations and cities inside the tent (climate's "non-state actor" architecture, internet governance's ICANN experiment) — the innovation forced by state-only gridlock.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> UN Charter (1945); Paris Agreement (2015); Keohane, After Hegemony (1984); Hale, Held & Young, Gridlock (2013).</p>
</TopicPage>
